WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:08.630
what do you think of when you first hear
the word mutant do you immediately

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:13.910
imagine cool supernatural powers? Sure,
these powers might make people mutants in

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:18.869
comic books. But, did you know that in our world we are all mutants? So, what do we

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:23.529
mean when we say the word mutant? And
what is a mutation? To answer this

00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:26.539
question, we need to go back to the
beginning of human life:

00:00:27.169 --> 00:00:32.140
the union of egg and sperm cells. Since
both egg and sperm cells contain DNA

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.789
we inherit half of our own DNA from each
parent. All of the DNA in a single

00:00:37.789 --> 00:00:43.019
person's body is called their genome, and
to find out how unique we really are, we

00:00:43.019 --> 00:00:47.969
have to compare our genomes to our
parents' genome. DNA is basically a set

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:52.309
of instructions that our cells use to
build proteins, which act like the cogs

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:53.749
and wheels within cells.

00:00:53.749 --> 00:00:58.429
Proteins influence the way our bodies
appear, function, and respond to our

00:00:58.429 --> 00:01:03.179
environment. The DNA instructions for
building proteins are spelled out using

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:11.050
four molecules called nucleotides: A, C, T
and G for short. These nucleotides are the

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.260
key to mutations. To be exact,

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:19.450
a mutation is a change in nucleotides, and it automatically translates to a change

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.720
in the protein product. Think of it like
a blueprint: when you change the

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.720
instructions, the final design will
change accordingly.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.090
most mutations occur during cell
division, when a mother cell fully

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.940
duplicates its DNA so that each of its daughter cells can receive a copy.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:40.400
Occasionally, mistakes can occur and one
copy of DNA that gets passed down might

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.590
be slightly different from the others.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.650
The DNA that contains the error is
called the mutated DNA.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.810
DNA mutations can occur in two different
ways. Somatic mutations occur in a

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.229
person's cells during their lifetime and
do not get passed down to future

00:01:55.229 --> 00:02:00.720
offspring. For example, smoking can cause somatic mutations which lead to cancer

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.530
and lung diseases. Hereditary mutations,
on the other hand, are locked into your

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:09.429
genes from the moment you're born. That's because they occur in a sperm or

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:13.500
egg cell of your parent before or
shortly after they fuse into an embryo,

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.870
the first cell that makes you. Hereditary
mutations are transmitted to you and

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.280
your children, because your embryo
becomes the template for all of your

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.480
cell development. When a hereditary
mutation happens for the first time in

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.320
your embryo it's called de novo, which
means "new" because it didn't exist in

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:36.050
your parents. A recent study estimates
that every one of us is born with about

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:42.290
60 do novo mutations. On the other hand,
you may inherit old hereditary mutations

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:47.390
from your ancestors. Both old and new
hereditary mutations will be passed down

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.160
to future generations,

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:54.150
each adding to the genetic variation
within a population. Genetic variation

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.130
consists of the unique family traits or
characteristics of a certain population

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:02.610
within a certain geographical region and
it's the reason we're not exactly the

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:06.920
same as our relatives. There is no one
else in this whole world who has the

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.890
exact same genome is yours. All thanks to mutations! Most hereditary mutations are

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:17.760
neutral--as far as we can tell, they have
no observable impact on health or outer

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:18.900
appearance.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:24.590
Mutations are an inherent part of life. So, why do we need them? If mutations did

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:30.239
not occur, we wouldn't have any new
genetic variation. After a while, everyone

00:03:30.239 --> 00:03:34.819
would be made up of a singular, dominant
genome. This would be fine as long as

00:03:34.819 --> 00:03:39.579
our environment stays the same. But our
environment is fickle, and nature selects

00:03:39.579 --> 00:03:43.120
which random mutations help us
survive as the world changes around us.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.530
This is how evolution works, and it
depends on the genetic variation

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:52.430
provided by mutations. Without genetic
variation, we wouldn't be able to adapt

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:57.310
to environmental changes. So, our
mutations don't necessarily give us

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.920
cool superpowers, but now you know they
give us the power to survive!

